Okay, I guess hearing no public speak, let's move on to item three, which is the budget workshop.

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:30.870
Item 3A is board and department presentations.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:34.630
Eric are you going to handle this for us basically do the run through?

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:42.790
>> I will several board of select members had asked to do it but they don't appear to have

00:01:44.465 --> 00:01:50.945
>> for one reason or other been able to do that. Of course, we start our meetings on time. I'm just making that point.

00:01:53.025 --> 00:01:53.905
Ouch.

00:01:54.065 --> 00:01:56.945
Okay. So We do have Jeff Murray here.

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:59.190
I thought that was Jeff.

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:00.790
Maybe

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:02.470
that's his iPhone.

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:10.630
>> So the the first thing I would like to ask is, is there anything particular

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:11.830
that you want to review

00:02:12.775 --> 00:02:14.615
or focus on

00:02:15.255 --> 00:02:17.415
before I give essentially

00:02:17.415 --> 00:02:20.055
a general overview of the budget?

00:02:23.495 --> 00:02:25.655
>> Anybody have anything particular for Eric?

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:36.380
I know you'd asked that question last week, I know that you know I talked about wanting to understand kind of what the impact was in the five year plan and somebody else had asked for

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:37.740
clarification

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:39.740
and some things or potential clarification

00:02:39.945 --> 00:02:41.705
remember exactly what that was.

00:02:45.145 --> 00:02:46.825
>> I just have a quick question.

00:02:47.385 --> 00:02:48.985
Shoot. How much

00:02:49.225 --> 00:02:54.680
in this budget year did did the town get for COVID relief?

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:58.120
Did the town get anything or does it all go 100% go to school?

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:02.360
>> No it doesn't 100% go to the school. I think the town

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:06.120
got a total of $31,000

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:07.800
in two

00:03:08.915 --> 00:03:10.915
total

00:03:10.995 --> 00:03:13.555
of which we have not spent

00:03:14.515 --> 00:03:15.795
very much of

00:03:16.275 --> 00:03:17.395
we have

00:03:17.715 --> 00:03:18.755
reimbursed.

00:03:18.755 --> 00:03:20.195
So we had asked

00:03:21.395 --> 00:03:25.460
various different departments within the town what their expenses

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:26.660
related.

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:32.500
So the only group that we've reimbursed that I know of is I know we've reimbursed

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:33.380
the library

00:03:33.885 --> 00:03:35.805
for their COVID related

00:03:35.805 --> 00:03:38.765
expenses which was basically putting up the shielding

00:03:38.765 --> 00:03:44.685
and a few other things that they were required to do before they reopened under the governor's executive orders.

00:03:44.765 --> 00:03:45.565
Okay.

00:03:45.805 --> 00:03:49.405
>> So we also spent about $4,000

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:50.180
out of that money

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:51.460
on

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:55.220
we put towards the combined finance

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:56.260
office

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:58.420
and equipping them

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:02.340
getting the IT support for them to be able to function

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:06.315
regardless of where which office they're in.

00:04:06.955 --> 00:04:07.755
That

00:04:07.755 --> 00:04:10.395
was one of the requests from the school

00:04:10.715 --> 00:04:12.955
that because they were

00:04:14.555 --> 00:04:18.075
took on the majority costs for acquisition of the software

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:23.370
that they asked us to reimburse them for that, which I thought was reasonable and agreed to.

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:28.970
>> We don't think we're as a town that we're going to get anything this year, right?

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:31.450
>> It does not look like it.

00:04:32.165 --> 00:04:38.325
The only funding that was identified early on for the town was ESSER funding

00:04:39.205 --> 00:04:42.645
which is strictly school related not town related.

00:04:42.885 --> 00:04:43.205
Okay.

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:49.190
And there's some question whether we're even going to get that. It sounds like maybe we're not.

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:50.310
Okay.

00:04:52.390 --> 00:04:54.950
>> So my suspicion is what we've got now

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:58.550
is what we're going to get in terms of that

00:04:59.925 --> 00:05:01.285
for funding.

00:05:03.685 --> 00:05:05.765
>> Eric, if I could say,

00:05:06.565 --> 00:05:12.885
we're going to need to find a storage area for all of the PPE and cleaning supplies that we have

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:16.400
stacked up in our meeting room at the fire station.

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:20.320
Most of this equipment will be received through our

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:23.120
EMS system,

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:26.880
and they made sure that us and the town were totally

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:27.600
prepared.

00:05:28.195 --> 00:05:28.995
PPE,

00:05:29.075 --> 00:05:30.035
cleansers,

00:05:30.035 --> 00:05:38.115
all the everything's there. We're gonna need to find a place to stockpile that for the next pandemic. That never happens, but

00:05:38.995 --> 00:05:42.600
it's gonna have to all get to one central location.

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:45.400
I'm sure the school has some also

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:49.480
and maybe even a library that we're going to have to stockpile somewhere.

00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:57.855
Just just just, you know, think about it and it's a ways off before it happens, but we're going to to do something. Sure.

00:05:58.495 --> 00:05:59.135
Okay.

00:06:00.815 --> 00:06:04.415
>> We'll put it in the new senior center building we're gonna build.

00:06:05.375 --> 00:06:08.015
Sure, you'll fill up the basement, why not? Yeah.

00:06:09.935 --> 00:06:11.215
>> Yeah, absolutely.

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:19.030
So the first thing I wanted to say is that if you look at the budget in broad strokes,

00:06:20.630 --> 00:06:21.590
last year

00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:23.830
there was quite

00:06:23.830 --> 00:06:25.030
a bit of uncertainty

00:06:25.705 --> 00:06:26.985
because

00:06:26.985 --> 00:06:31.865
we were hiring a number of people and whenever you're hiring a bunch of new people,

00:06:32.185 --> 00:06:43.010
you know, didn't really know where we were going to end up with benefits for them because it obviously makes a big difference between whether they're single or they have a family plan or whatnot.

00:06:43.090 --> 00:06:47.650
So we ended up better off than we anticipated,

00:06:49.730 --> 00:06:52.050
you know, when we did the actual hiring.

00:06:52.785 --> 00:06:56.145
And we also were under on salary

00:06:56.625 --> 00:07:03.825
in part because the hiring process takes a while. So there's always a gap in time when you're not paying for

00:07:05.585 --> 00:07:06.225
an

00:07:06.865 --> 00:07:07.265
employee.

00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:10.250
So overall on salary

00:07:10.490 --> 00:07:12.410
and also I declined

00:07:14.090 --> 00:07:15.770
to advertise

00:07:15.770 --> 00:07:18.010
and hire a separate

00:07:18.490 --> 00:07:21.850
senior services coordinator which we built into that budget.

00:07:22.445 --> 00:07:23.245
I

00:07:23.245 --> 00:07:26.045
did that for two reasons. One, because of COVID

00:07:26.205 --> 00:07:29.485
we weren't really able to offer any programming

00:07:29.965 --> 00:07:32.045
and two, because I think

00:07:32.445 --> 00:07:35.005
it actually works pretty well

00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:38.810
keeping that assignment with the assistant assessor.

00:07:39.130 --> 00:07:40.090
It's

00:07:40.090 --> 00:07:43.290
a fair bit cheaper to the town, number one.

00:07:44.170 --> 00:07:45.290
And secondly,

00:07:46.650 --> 00:07:46.970
the

00:07:48.815 --> 00:07:51.455
times when you need the most support there

00:07:52.015 --> 00:07:57.295
are different than the times when the assessor's office is working the hardest.

00:07:57.775 --> 00:08:01.215
So there's periods of time when the

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:04.400
assessor's office is 100% occupied

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:06.560
and there's times when they have slack

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:10.000
so it works well as a combo job

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:11.680
there.

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:15.600
The second thing is that with the combination

00:08:16.755 --> 00:08:17.395
of

00:08:17.795 --> 00:08:19.795
the finance office,

00:08:19.795 --> 00:08:22.355
the town is now really only required

00:08:22.675 --> 00:08:24.115
to supply

00:08:24.275 --> 00:08:27.075
salary and benefits to one individual

00:08:27.315 --> 00:08:30.035
and even though Marina makes more

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:33.290
than her predecessor at the assistant role

00:08:33.530 --> 00:08:34.650
considerably

00:08:34.650 --> 00:08:35.450
than

00:08:35.530 --> 00:08:37.130
Maureen Broncado,

00:08:37.450 --> 00:08:44.250
she also brings a lot more capability to the job number one and two now we're paying for one person instead of two.

00:08:44.745 --> 00:08:48.745
So that has been the savings that we're able to project through

00:08:49.145 --> 00:08:50.425
in this budget.

00:08:51.145 --> 00:08:54.185
And the other thing is that

00:08:54.505 --> 00:08:56.185
we were able to

00:08:56.680 --> 00:08:57.480
further

00:08:59.000 --> 00:08:59.880
reduce

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:01.640
the expenses

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:04.040
in the tax collector's office.

00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:05.960
Basically,

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:07.640
we've

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:12.360
kind of worked out, we're still in union negotiations

00:09:12.015 --> 00:09:15.695
but we're gonna, that office is gonna end up going down to

00:09:16.015 --> 00:09:19.535
average twenty six hours a week from thirty four.

00:09:20.655 --> 00:09:23.935
And there's gonna be a change in essentially a change in benefits

00:09:24.490 --> 00:09:30.410
and a change in hourly rate. But the net savings to the town is about $12,000

00:09:30.410 --> 00:09:34.650
over what we were doing before. So that's fairly significant

00:09:35.930 --> 00:09:37.770
from the town's perspective.

00:09:38.955 --> 00:09:40.635
The cost of insurance

00:09:40.715 --> 00:09:42.715
was fairly flat,

00:09:43.515 --> 00:09:45.515
although that also

00:09:47.755 --> 00:09:48.635
includes

00:09:48.635 --> 00:09:52.155
the fact that Kerma has dropped

00:09:51.570 --> 00:09:53.970
their cyber liability policy.

00:09:54.530 --> 00:09:56.610
So yes, their costs

00:09:57.570 --> 00:09:59.490
didn't go up, but

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:01.730
the benefit

00:10:01.730 --> 00:10:03.410
portion of it dropped.

00:10:03.730 --> 00:10:06.530
So that's something we may have to revisit

00:10:06.935 --> 00:10:09.735
at some point in the past.

00:10:10.215 --> 00:10:11.255
The bottom

00:10:11.255 --> 00:10:14.295
line is for the town's

00:10:14.535 --> 00:10:17.015
what I call the operating budget,

00:10:19.335 --> 00:10:21.815
which is the town budget

00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:23.640
not including

00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:24.920
school,

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:27.080
the cost of education,

00:10:27.640 --> 00:10:32.520
and then not including what we are putting into capital funds

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:33.560
is

00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:36.120
decreasing about 1.5%.

00:10:37.225 --> 00:10:38.585
And that's

00:10:38.665 --> 00:10:42.505
due to a number of factors. We were able to tighten up a few things,

00:10:45.065 --> 00:10:45.945
you know, and

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:50.600
yeah

00:10:50.920 --> 00:10:56.600
yeah so so that's that's kind of the the town general operating budget.

00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:01.080
Where we did go up was in the capital funding

00:11:01.395 --> 00:11:04.755
which is basically roads and bridges and culverts

00:11:04.755 --> 00:11:06.595
and physical infrastructure.

00:11:07.475 --> 00:11:10.355
As I talked fairly extensively last year,

00:11:10.595 --> 00:11:14.675
you know, our biggest single issue in the town is

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:18.700
the just the fact that we've we kind of underfunded

00:11:18.700 --> 00:11:20.460
the physical infrastructure

00:11:20.620 --> 00:11:22.140
for a bunch of years

00:11:22.460 --> 00:11:23.340
and

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:25.420
it's gotten to the point where it's

00:11:25.820 --> 00:11:27.500
it's you

00:11:27.500 --> 00:11:29.260
know it needs some serious attention.

00:11:29.985 --> 00:11:31.025
We're

00:11:31.025 --> 00:11:32.625
assuming we're pushing

00:11:33.505 --> 00:11:35.265
out the work we're doing

00:11:35.585 --> 00:11:43.185
and the intention is to kind of deal with it over the next fifteen plus years so it's not a big hit all at once.

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:49.880
Although I know the Board of Selectmen does want to consider bonding for some of the projects

00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:51.960
to accelerate that.

00:11:52.520 --> 00:11:55.400
We did get a kind of final

00:11:55.640 --> 00:11:58.520
wrap up report on all the town culverts

00:11:59.015 --> 00:12:02.375
and kind of the bottom line with the culverts is

00:12:02.695 --> 00:12:05.175
we have five we need to address

00:12:06.055 --> 00:12:07.495
relatively

00:12:07.495 --> 00:12:10.135
quickly. Two we should probably be addressing

00:12:10.375 --> 00:12:12.135
as fast as we can fund

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:15.080
and three more that definitely

00:12:15.080 --> 00:12:16.680
need to be done within,

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:22.600
you know, say the next ten years. So that's kind of where we're looking in terms of culverts.

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:26.520
Right now we have some money set aside

00:12:27.245 --> 00:12:29.725
for the Bunker Hill Bridge

00:12:30.205 --> 00:12:32.845
and we have some more money

00:12:33.085 --> 00:12:36.205
that we're proposing to put into that this year,

00:12:36.685 --> 00:12:39.885
which should probably leave about $120,000

00:12:39.885 --> 00:12:40.845
to fund

00:12:42.070 --> 00:12:44.070
the budget after this one.

00:12:44.870 --> 00:12:47.670
So that project in total should

00:12:48.950 --> 00:12:52.230
cost the town somewhere around $400,000

00:12:52.230 --> 00:12:55.670
We'll have a better guess or we'll have a better estimate

00:12:56.235 --> 00:12:59.915
when the engineering firm has completed

00:13:00.075 --> 00:13:02.155
what they call the 30%

00:13:02.955 --> 00:13:04.475
portion of the bridge,

00:13:04.715 --> 00:13:07.515
which means the design is 30% complete

00:13:08.315 --> 00:13:12.150
and they have they they're ready to

00:13:12.310 --> 00:13:13.750
basically present

00:13:13.750 --> 00:13:15.110
and we're going to pick

00:13:15.270 --> 00:13:18.150
which way we go in terms of bridge design.

00:13:20.550 --> 00:13:24.230
And that has been delayed basically because

00:13:24.390 --> 00:13:26.470
the state

00:13:27.885 --> 00:13:30.205
didn't finish surveying

00:13:30.525 --> 00:13:31.405
before

00:13:31.405 --> 00:13:32.125
the

00:13:33.165 --> 00:13:38.765
it got too cold and since one of the things they have to do is kind of contour the riverbed

00:13:38.850 --> 00:13:42.130
which involves somebody standing in waders in the river

00:13:42.930 --> 00:13:43.810
with

00:13:43.810 --> 00:13:48.050
the survey poll they have elected to put that off till it's warmer

00:13:48.370 --> 00:13:50.050
which is a little unfortunate.

00:13:51.410 --> 00:13:54.210
We're moving ahead with some aspects of the design

00:13:54.735 --> 00:13:57.855
but we're definitely going to be a little delayed,

00:13:58.335 --> 00:14:03.215
but probably not so much that we can't anticipate a start date next spring.

00:14:04.895 --> 00:14:07.055
As far as the roadwork goes,

00:14:07.730 --> 00:14:09.970
I've been working with Jay

00:14:10.370 --> 00:14:11.970
on the presentation

00:14:12.370 --> 00:14:15.890
of kind of what we're planning on doing for roadwork.

00:14:16.770 --> 00:14:18.210
And we're keeping

00:14:18.210 --> 00:14:19.490
very much the same

00:14:20.225 --> 00:14:28.465
motif that we did last year other than you know we're just we're changing up the order we do things a little bit

00:14:28.705 --> 00:14:29.505
and

00:14:30.305 --> 00:14:32.545
you know we're trying to get a little more

00:14:33.230 --> 00:14:34.750
you know economical

00:14:34.750 --> 00:14:38.670
with it. I mean the reality is you know we're

00:14:38.910 --> 00:14:46.190
many millions of dollars in the hole on road work because we underfunded that for you know twenty plus years.

00:14:47.015 --> 00:14:51.495
And so what we're looking at doing is that what kind of compromises

00:14:51.495 --> 00:14:52.615
can we make

00:14:52.855 --> 00:14:56.775
because some of our roads are bad enough that you know they're

00:14:57.015 --> 00:15:04.070
you know the potholes are substantial enough that that they're you know they're it affects their drivability.

00:15:04.550 --> 00:15:09.590
So we're looking at you know essentially chip sealing doing a lot of chip sealing

00:15:09.750 --> 00:15:17.345
but doing a lot of shimming and filling in some of the really bad depressions and kind of doing it systematically

00:15:17.585 --> 00:15:25.265
and then doing a section of the town each year with the goal of you know in the next four or five years at least getting all the

00:15:26.385 --> 00:15:27.905
major defects

00:15:28.210 --> 00:15:34.610
and then putting out nice cat by the way and then putting off the most

00:15:35.810 --> 00:15:37.010
reconstruction

00:15:37.010 --> 00:15:37.730
work

00:15:38.130 --> 00:15:46.935
because that's so expensive we're going to have to do just a very small amount of it each year just because the cost per mile to reconstruct

00:15:46.935 --> 00:15:50.055
a road is somewhere around $300

00:15:50.455 --> 00:15:54.695
and we just don't have the funds to reconstruct a lot of what we need to.

00:15:55.815 --> 00:15:58.055
Other than that in terms of capital,

00:15:58.720 --> 00:15:59.600
we're

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:02.400
the Board of Selectmen is proposing

00:16:02.560 --> 00:16:03.360
to

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:05.680
fund the fire department

00:16:05.920 --> 00:16:07.680
at $75,000

00:16:07.680 --> 00:16:09.440
for their capital equipment,

00:16:09.920 --> 00:16:13.200
which was less than what we originally requested,

00:16:13.855 --> 00:16:16.895
but there's currently about $33,000

00:16:16.895 --> 00:16:18.735
in that account right now,

00:16:19.295 --> 00:16:25.375
so that is enough that we should be able to purchase everything that was on their priority list.

00:16:26.975 --> 00:16:33.570
You know we'll run the account back down to zero but they'll have the stuff they need to to work with,

00:16:33.970 --> 00:16:34.850
for now.

00:16:38.530 --> 00:16:45.955
>> What else? Eric, is Eric, me on that? You're talking about the fir the first three of the five on the priority list.

00:16:46.355 --> 00:16:46.995
Correct?

00:16:47.795 --> 00:16:51.715
Correct. Yeah. There's five items that the long term,

